(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[22] = {"n":22,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 30% · B2 50% · B2+ 20%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Exoplanets","p":["For centuries, people have wondered whether other stars have planets of their own, and whether any of those planets might support life. Until recently, this was a question for philosophers rather than scientists, because planets around other stars, known as exoplanets, are far too faint and distant to be seen directly with ordinary telescopes. A planet reflects only a tiny amount of light, and it is usually lost in the glare of the star it orbits, much like a firefly flying next to a powerful searchlight.","The first confirmed discoveries came in the 1990s. In 1995, two Swiss astronomers, Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz, detected a giant planet orbiting a Sun-like star called 51 Pegasi. Their discovery, which later earned them a Nobel Prize, surprised many scientists: the planet was about half the mass of Jupiter, yet it circled its star in just four days, far closer than Mercury is to our Sun. Such 'hot Jupiters' showed that other planetary systems could be very different from our own.","Since then, more than five thousand exoplanets have been confirmed, mostly using two indirect methods. The first, the radial velocity method, relies on the fact that a planet's gravity causes its star to wobble slightly. As the star moves towards and away from us, the light it emits shifts very slightly in colour, and sensitive instruments can measure this change. The second, the transit method, detects the small, regular dip in a star's brightness that occurs when a planet passes in front of it. NASA's Kepler space telescope, launched in 2009, used this method to discover thousands of planets.","These discoveries have revealed an astonishing variety of worlds. Some planets are larger than Jupiter; others are rocky worlds slightly bigger than Earth, a type known as 'super-Earths', which does not exist in our own solar system. Some orbit two stars at once, while others travel alone through space without any star at all. Researchers now estimate that most stars in our galaxy have at least one planet, which means that there may be hundreds of billions of planets in the Milky Way alone.","Of particular interest are planets located in the so-called habitable zone, the region around a star where temperatures would allow liquid water to exist on a planet's surface. Since all known life depends on water, these planets are considered the best places to search for life. However, being in the habitable zone does not guarantee that a planet is suitable for life; it would also need, among other things, a stable atmosphere and protection from harmful radiation.","The next step is to study the atmospheres of exoplanets. When a planet passes in front of its star, some of the starlight travels through the planet's atmosphere, and different gases absorb particular colours of light. By analysing this light, the James Webb Space Telescope has already detected water vapour and carbon dioxide on distant planets. Scientists hope that, in the coming decades, they may identify combinations of gases that could only be produced by living organisms. If they succeed, it would be one of the most significant discoveries in human history."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how the solar system was formed","the life of two Swiss astronomers","how scientists find and study planets around other stars","why Jupiter is the largest planet"],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"Every paragraph discusses exoplanets.","h":"الكواكب الخارجية."},{"n":2,"s":"Why does the writer compare a planet to a firefly next to a searchlight?","o":["to show that planets produce their own light","to explain how telescopes work","to describe the colour of distant planets","to show how hard it is to see a planet near a bright star"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"The planet's light is lost in the star's glare.","h":"صعوبة رؤية الكوكب."},{"n":3,"s":"Why was the planet orbiting 51 Pegasi surprising?","o":["It was smaller than Mercury.","It was a giant planet very close to its star.","It had no star at all.","It was covered in water."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"It circled its star in four days.","h":"كوكب ضخم قريب جدًا من نجمه."},{"n":4,"s":"The word 'wobble' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["grow larger","become brighter","move slightly from side to side","stop moving"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The star moves slightly back and forth.","h":"يتمايل."},{"n":5,"s":"How does the transit method detect planets?","o":["by measuring a small dip in a star's brightness","by photographing the planet directly","by listening to radio signals","by measuring the planet's temperature"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"انخفاض سطوع النجم."},{"n":6,"s":"What can be inferred about 'super-Earths'?","o":["They are larger than Jupiter.","They always orbit two stars.","No planet of this kind orbits our Sun.","They were discovered before 1995."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"They do not exist in our solar system.","h":"لا يوجد مثلها في نظامنا."},{"n":7,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'which' in 'which means that there may be hundreds of billions' refers to","o":["the Milky Way","the estimate that most stars have at least one planet","planets without stars","the planet Jupiter"],"k":1,"ty":"reference","e":"It refers to the whole preceding idea.","h":"يعود على التقدير."},{"n":8,"s":"According to the passage, why are planets in the habitable zone important?","o":["They are the easiest planets to see.","They are always rocky.","They have no atmosphere.","Liquid water could exist on their surfaces."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"إمكانية وجود الماء السائل."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as necessary for a planet to support life?","o":["a large moon","liquid water","a stable atmosphere","protection from radiation"],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"A moon is not mentioned.","h":"القمر غير مذكور."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards the search for life on exoplanets?","o":["certain that life will never be found","critical of the cost","hopeful about its possible importance","uninterested in the results"],"k":2,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"It would be 'one of the most significant discoveries'.","h":"متفائل."}]},{"t":"The House of Wisdom in Baghdad","p":["In the late eighth century, the Abbasid caliphs founded a new capital, Baghdad, on the banks of the River Tigris. Within a few decades, it had become one of the largest and wealthiest cities in the world and a centre of trade linking Asia, Africa and Europe. It also became a centre of learning. The introduction of papermaking from Central Asia made books far cheaper than before, and bookshops and private libraries spread throughout the city. At the heart of this intellectual life was an institution known as Bayt al-Hikma, or the House of Wisdom.","Historians still debate exactly what the House of Wisdom was. It probably began as a royal library under Caliph Harun al-Rashid, and it seems to have grown significantly under his son, al-Ma'mun, who ruled from 813 to 833. Al-Ma'mun was deeply interested in philosophy and science, and he supported scholars generously. Some accounts claim that he sent missions to the Byzantine Empire to obtain Greek manuscripts, and that he even paid translators the weight of their books in gold, although this story may have been exaggerated.","The most famous activity associated with the House of Wisdom was translation. Scholars translated works of philosophy, mathematics, astronomy and medicine from Greek, Persian, Syriac and Sanskrit into Arabic. Among the translators was Hunayn ibn Ishaq, a Christian physician who translated many works by the Greek doctor Galen and developed careful methods for comparing different manuscripts. This translation movement preserved many ancient texts that might otherwise have been lost and made Arabic the leading language of science for several centuries.","However, the scholars of Baghdad did far more than copy the ideas of earlier civilizations. They tested, corrected and extended them. The mathematician Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi wrote a book on solving equations whose title gave us the word 'algebra', and his own name, in Latin form, gave us the word 'algorithm'. Astronomers built observatories, measured the length of a degree of the Earth's circumference with impressive accuracy and produced improved astronomical tables. The Banu Musa brothers wrote a famous book describing ingenious mechanical devices. Physicians, meanwhile, wrote detailed medical encyclopedias and emphasized careful observation of patients, an approach that would later influence hospitals far beyond the Islamic world.","The influence of this work spread far beyond Baghdad. From the eleventh and twelfth centuries onwards, many Arabic texts were translated into Latin, particularly in Spain and Sicily, where Muslim, Christian and Jewish scholars lived and worked side by side. These translations introduced European scholars to Hindu-Arabic numerals, including the concept of zero, as well as to advances in medicine, optics and astronomy, and they played an important part in the later development of European science.","In 1258, Baghdad was captured and destroyed by Mongol armies. According to some accounts, so many books were thrown into the Tigris that its water turned black with ink. Whether or not this description is accurate, the destruction marked the end of Baghdad's golden age. Yet the ideas developed there survived in libraries from Cairo to Cordoba and continue to influence science today. The House of Wisdom remains a powerful symbol of how openness to knowledge from different cultures can lead to remarkable progress."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["The Mongol Invasion of Asia","How to Translate Ancient Books","A Centre of Knowledge in Abbasid Baghdad","The Geography of the River Tigris"],"k":2,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage focuses on the House of Wisdom.","h":"مركز المعرفة في بغداد."},{"n":12,"s":"What does the writer suggest about the story of translators being paid in gold?","o":["It is confirmed by many documents.","It shows al-Ma'mun disliked translation.","It happened under Harun al-Rashid.","It may not be completely accurate."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"'this story may have been exaggerated'.","h":"قد تكون مبالغة."},{"n":13,"s":"Who was Hunayn ibn Ishaq?","o":["a physician who translated Greek medical works","a caliph who founded Baghdad","a mathematician who invented algebra","a Mongol leader"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 describes him.","h":"طبيب ومترجم."},{"n":14,"s":"Why was the translation movement important?","o":["It replaced Arabic with Greek.","It ended scientific research in Baghdad.","It preserved ancient texts and made Arabic a language of science.","It was the only activity of the House of Wisdom."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains its importance.","h":"حفظ النصوص ونشر العلم بالعربية."},{"n":15,"s":"What is the main purpose of paragraph 4?","o":["to describe the life of al-Ma'mun","to show that Baghdad's scholars made original contributions","to explain how books were copied","to compare Baghdad with Cairo"],"k":1,"ty":"insertion / paragraph function","e":"It begins 'did far more than copy'.","h":"إظهار الإسهامات الأصيلة."},{"n":16,"s":"The word 'ingenious' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["very old","cleverly designed","extremely heavy","dangerous"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Clever mechanical devices.","h":"بارع."},{"n":17,"s":"According to the passage, which words come from al-Khwarizmi's work?","o":["astronomy and optics","zero and numeral","library and wisdom","algebra and algorithm"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 states this.","h":"الجبر والخوارزمية."},{"n":18,"s":"Why does the writer mention Spain and Sicily?","o":["They were where the House of Wisdom was founded.","They were places where Arabic texts were translated into Latin.","They were captured by the Mongols.","They produced the first observatories."],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"مراكز الترجمة إلى اللاتينية."},{"n":19,"s":"Which of the following happened LAST?","o":["Baghdad was destroyed by Mongol armies.","Al-Ma'mun became caliph.","Baghdad was founded as a capital.","Hunayn ibn Ishaq translated Galen."],"k":0,"ty":"sequence","e":"1258 is the latest event.","h":"١٢٥٨ هو الأخير."},{"n":20,"s":"What is the writer's main conclusion?","o":["Openness to knowledge from other cultures can lead to great progress.","Libraries are no longer necessary.","Translation is less important than original research.","Baghdad's ideas were completely lost."],"k":0,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Stated in the last sentence.","h":"الانفتاح على المعرفة."}]},{"t":"How sinkholes form","p":["Every few years, dramatic images appear in the news of roads, cars or even houses suddenly disappearing into enormous holes in the ground. These holes, called sinkholes, can appear almost overnight and may be tens of metres wide and deep. Although they can seem mysterious, sinkholes are a natural geological process, and scientists have a good understanding of why and where they form.","Most sinkholes occur in areas where the rock beneath the surface can be dissolved by water. Such rocks include limestone, gypsum and salt. Rainwater naturally absorbs a small amount of carbon dioxide from the air and soil, which makes it slightly acidic. As this water seeps into the ground through cracks, it slowly dissolves the rock, widening the cracks into channels and, over thousands of years, creating underground caves. Landscapes shaped in this way are known as karst landscapes. On the surface, they often have few rivers, because rainwater quickly disappears underground, but beneath the surface there may be complex networks of tunnels and caves, some large enough for people to walk through.","Geologists distinguish between several types of sinkholes. In solution sinkholes, the rock at the surface is dissolved gradually, forming a shallow bowl-shaped depression. Cover-subsidence sinkholes form where loose sand covers the rock; the sand slowly trickles down into the openings below, creating a gentle dip. The most dangerous kind, however, is the cover-collapse sinkhole. Here, a layer of clay or soil forms a bridge over an underground cavity. The cavity grows larger over time, but the surface shows no sign of the danger until the bridge can no longer support its own weight and suddenly collapses.","Although sinkholes are natural, human activity can make them more likely. Pumping large quantities of groundwater for farming or drinking can lower the water table, removing the support that water provided to underground cavities. Leaking water pipes, poorly designed drainage systems and construction work can also concentrate water in particular places and speed up erosion. Heavy rain after a long drought is a common trigger, because the sudden weight of water and the rapid flow can cause weakened ground to give way. In some mining areas, the removal of underground material has also created empty spaces that later collapse.","Sinkholes are found all over the world. Florida in the United States is famous for them, and the Dead Sea region has seen thousands appear in recent decades as the sea's water level has fallen. The Arabian Peninsula also contains karst areas. In the Al-Summan Plateau, north-east of Riyadh, there are many natural caves and sinkholes, some of which have been explored by cave specialists and contain impressive rock formations.","Because cover-collapse sinkholes give little warning, preventing damage depends largely on investigation and planning. Before building in karst areas, engineers can use ground-penetrating radar and test drilling to locate hidden cavities. Governments may also restrict groundwater pumping and require careful management of drainage. Residents can watch for warning signs, such as cracks in walls, doors that suddenly stick or small depressions in the ground. While sinkholes cannot always be prevented, understanding the processes behind them can greatly reduce the risks they pose."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how caves are explored","the history of Florida","how to build stronger roads","the causes, types and risks of sinkholes"],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains sinkholes in detail.","h":"الحفر الانهيارية."},{"n":22,"s":"Why is rainwater able to dissolve certain rocks?","o":["It is extremely hot underground.","It contains large amounts of salt.","It becomes slightly acidic by absorbing carbon dioxide.","It moves very quickly through the ground."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"الماء حمضي قليلًا."},{"n":23,"s":"The word 'seeps' in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["falls heavily","freezes","evaporates","flows slowly through small openings"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Water passes slowly through cracks.","h":"يتسرب."},{"n":24,"s":"What is a karst landscape?","o":["land shaped by rock being dissolved by water","a sandy desert with no caves","land formed by volcanoes","a coastal area with high cliffs"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 defines it.","h":"تضاريس الكارست."},{"n":25,"s":"Why are cover-collapse sinkholes especially dangerous?","o":["They give little or no warning before they collapse.","They are always very shallow.","They only form in cities.","They are filled with acid."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"The surface shows no sign of danger.","h":"لا تعطي إنذارًا."},{"n":26,"s":"In paragraph 3, the word 'its' in 'support its own weight' refers to","o":["the cavity","the sand","the bridge of clay or soil","the rock"],"k":2,"ty":"reference","e":"The bridge cannot support its weight.","h":"تعود على الجسر الطيني."},{"n":27,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a human activity that can cause sinkholes?","o":["pumping groundwater","planting trees","leaking water pipes","construction work"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Planting trees is not mentioned.","h":"زراعة الأشجار غير مذكورة."},{"n":28,"s":"What can be inferred about the Dead Sea region?","o":["Falling water levels have contributed to sinkholes there.","It has no karst rocks.","Sinkholes there are very old.","Its sinkholes are caused by earthquakes."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"Thousands appeared as the water level fell.","h":"انخفاض المياه سبب."},{"n":29,"s":"Why does the writer mention the Al-Summan Plateau?","o":["to warn people not to travel to Riyadh","to describe a famous building","to compare it with Florida's weather","to show that sinkholes and caves also exist in Saudi Arabia"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"It gives a local example.","h":"مثال محلي."},{"n":30,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["It tells the story of one disaster.","It compares two countries' laws.","It argues against building in cities.","It explains a process, its types, causes and ways to reduce risk."],"k":3,"ty":"organization","e":"The paragraphs follow this order.","h":"عملية وأنواع وأسباب وحلول."}]},{"t":"Language and thought","p":["Does the language we speak influence the way we think? This question has fascinated linguists, psychologists and philosophers for more than a century. The idea that it does is often associated with the American linguist Benjamin Lee Whorf, who argued in the 1930s and 1940s that speakers of different languages experience the world in fundamentally different ways. In its strongest form, this idea, sometimes called linguistic determinism, suggests that language limits thought, so that people cannot think about concepts for which their language has no words.","Few researchers today accept this strong version. People regularly understand ideas for which they have no single word, and new words are constantly invented to describe new things. Arabic speakers, for instance, had no traditional word for 'computer' until one was needed, but this did not prevent them from understanding computers. Moreover, babies and animals clearly think without language at all. Experiments show, for example, that infants who cannot yet speak are surprised when objects seem to disappear or pass through one another, which suggests that they already have expectations about the physical world. Thought, then, does not depend entirely on words.","However, a weaker version of the idea, known as linguistic relativity, has received considerable support from experiments. This view holds that language does not determine thought but can influence what people notice, remember and find easy to think about. One famous example concerns colour. Russian has two separate basic words for light blue and dark blue. In experiments, Russian speakers were slightly faster than English speakers at distinguishing between these shades, suggesting that their language had sharpened their attention to the difference.","Another striking case involves direction. Speakers of Guugu Yimithirr, an Aboriginal language in Australia, do not use words such as 'left' and 'right' to describe locations. Instead, they use compass directions, saying, for example, that a cup is to the north of a plate. As a result, they appear to maintain a constant awareness of direction, even indoors or in unfamiliar places, an ability that most speakers of other languages lack.","Grammar may also have effects. Some languages require speakers to state how they know something, for example whether they saw it themselves or heard about it from someone else. Others assign grammatical gender to nouns, and some studies have suggested that this can subtly affect how speakers describe objects. In one study, speakers of languages in which the word for 'bridge' is feminine were more likely to describe bridges as 'elegant', while speakers of languages in which it is masculine tended to choose words such as 'strong'. However, other researchers have failed to repeat some of these results, so they should be treated with caution.","Overall, the current evidence suggests that language and thought interact in complex ways. Language is not a prison that prevents us from thinking certain thoughts, but it may act more like a set of habits, guiding our attention in particular directions. This has practical implications: learning a new language may not only allow us to communicate with more people but also give us new ways of seeing the world."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["whether and how language influences thinking","the history of the Arabic language","how to learn a foreign language quickly","the life of Benjamin Lee Whorf"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage discusses the language–thought debate.","h":"علاقة اللغة بالتفكير."},{"n":32,"s":"What does linguistic determinism claim?","o":["Language has no effect on thought.","All languages have the same words.","Language limits what people are able to think.","Animals can learn human languages."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 defines it.","h":"اللغة تحدد التفكير."},{"n":33,"s":"Why does the writer mention the Arabic word for 'computer'?","o":["to show that Arabic has few modern words","to show that people can understand ideas before having a word for them","to explain how computers were invented","to support linguistic determinism"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"It is evidence against the strong version.","h":"دليل ضد الحتمية اللغوية."},{"n":34,"s":"The word 'sharpened' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["made weaker","confused","replaced","made more precise"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Language made their attention more precise.","h":"شحذ."},{"n":35,"s":"What did the colour experiment show?","o":["English speakers could not see blue.","Russian speakers distinguished two shades of blue slightly faster.","Russian has no word for blue.","Colour has no connection with language."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 describes the result.","h":"تمييز أسرع للأزرق."},{"n":36,"s":"What can be inferred about speakers of Guugu Yimithirr?","o":["They cannot find their way indoors.","They usually know which direction they are facing.","They use 'left' and 'right' very often.","They live only in cities."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"They maintain a constant awareness of direction.","h":"يعرفون الاتجاهات دائمًا."},{"n":37,"s":"Why does the writer say some results about grammatical gender 'should be treated with caution'?","o":["Other researchers have not been able to repeat them.","They were published long ago.","They only involved children.","They were about colours, not objects."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"لم تتكرر النتائج."},{"n":38,"s":"In paragraph 6, 'it' in 'it may act more like a set of habits' refers to","o":["thought","a prison","the evidence","language"],"k":3,"ty":"reference","e":"Language may act like habits.","h":"تعود على اللغة."},{"n":39,"s":"Which of the following is NOT discussed as an area where language may affect thought?","o":["colour","direction","musical ability","grammatical gender"],"k":2,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Music is not discussed.","h":"الموسيقى غير مذكورة."},{"n":40,"s":"Which statement best summarizes the writer's conclusion?","o":["Language completely controls how we think.","Language influences thought in complex ways but does not imprison it.","Language and thought are unrelated.","Only some languages influence thought."],"k":1,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"The final paragraph states this.","h":"تأثير معقد لا قيد."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"Rarely ______ such a talented young musician perform in public.","o":["I have seen","I saw","have I seen","did I saw"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"have I seen — Rarely at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Rarely."},{"n":42,"s":"You ______ have brought any food; the organizers had already prepared lunch for everyone.","o":["mustn't","couldn't","shouldn't to","needn't"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"needn't — needn't have = it was unnecessary but you did it.","h":"needn't have."},{"n":43,"s":"The children were excited about ______ to the science museum.","o":["being taken","taking","be taken","been taken"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"being taken — Passive gerund after a preposition.","h":"اسم الفعل المبني للمجهول."},{"n":44,"s":"We finally got the air conditioner ______ after three phone calls.","o":["repaired","repair","to repair","repairing"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"repaired — get + object + past participle.","h":"get something done."},{"n":45,"s":"This time next week, we ______ over the Atlantic on our way to the conference.","o":["will fly","are flown","have flown","will be flying"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will be flying — Future continuous for an action in progress.","h":"المستقبل المستمر."},{"n":46,"s":"______ completed the experiment, the students wrote their reports.","o":["Have","Had","Having","To have"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Having — Perfect participle clause.","h":"Having + p.p."},{"n":47,"s":"______ you won a million riyals, what would you do with the money?","o":["Unless","Supposing","Provided that","In case"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Supposing — Supposing = imagine that.","h":"Supposing."},{"n":48,"s":"You had better leave now; ______, you will miss the last bus.","o":["unless","otherwise","however","therefore"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"otherwise — otherwise = if not.","h":"otherwise."},{"n":49,"s":"He talks about the project as if he ______ it all by himself.","o":["has done","does","had done","would do"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had done — as if + past perfect for an unreal past.","h":"as if."},{"n":50,"s":"I ______ warn you that the road may be closed tomorrow.","o":["do","am","have","did to"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"do — Emphatic do.","h":"التوكيد بـ do."},{"n":51,"s":"The question is ______ the government will approve the new budget.","o":["if or not","whether","that","what"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"whether — Noun clause with whether.","h":"whether."},{"n":52,"s":"The president ______ visit the new hospital next Monday, according to the official schedule.","o":["is going","will to","has to be","is to"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is to — be to for official plans.","h":"be to."},{"n":53,"s":"No sooner had the guests arrived ______ the power went out.","o":["when","than","then","that"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"than — No sooner … than.","h":"No sooner … than."},{"n":54,"s":"If the earth ______ to stop rotating, one side would be extremely hot.","o":["was being","is","had","were"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"were — were to for an imaginary future.","h":"were to."},{"n":55,"s":"The applicants ______ for the interview should arrive fifteen minutes early.","o":["selecting","who selected","to select","selected"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"selected — Reduced relative clause (passive).","h":"اختصار جملة الصلة."},{"n":56,"s":"This is the village ______ my grandfather spent his childhood.","o":["which","that","where","whose"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"where — where for place.","h":"where."},{"n":57,"s":"She is accustomed ______ long hours in the hospital.","o":["to working","to work","working","for working"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to working — accustomed to + -ing.","h":"accustomed to + -ing."},{"n":58,"s":"The team ______ have won if their best player hadn't been injured.","o":["must","might","can","shall"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"might — might have for past possibility.","h":"might have."},{"n":59,"s":"Scarcely ______ the bell rung when the students rushed out.","o":["did","was","has","had"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had — Scarcely + had + subject + p.p.","h":"Scarcely had."},{"n":60,"s":"The documents need ______ before they can be sent to the embassy.","o":["translate","translating","translated","to translating"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"translating — need + -ing with a passive meaning.","h":"need + -ing."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"The (A) amount of tourists (B) visiting AlUla (C) has (D) grown rapidly.","o":["amount","visiting","has","grown"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"amount — Tourists are countable: number.","h":"number مع المعدود."},{"n":62,"s":"She (A) suggested (B) to visit the museum (C) before (D) lunch.","o":["suggested","to visit","before","lunch"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"to visit — suggest + -ing: visiting.","h":"suggest + -ing."},{"n":63,"s":"The professor, together (A) with his (B) students, (C) are attending (D) the conference.","o":["with","students","are","the"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"are — together with does not change the subject: is.","h":"الفاعل مفرد."},{"n":64,"s":"If he (A) would have (B) studied harder, he (C) would have (D) passed.","o":["would have","studied","would have","passed"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"would have — Third conditional: If he had studied.","h":"If + had."},{"n":65,"s":"The museum (A) which we visited (B) last week (C) it was (D) fascinating.","o":["which","last","it","fascinating"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"it — Remove 'it': the subject is already 'The museum'.","h":"حذف it."},{"n":66,"s":"(A) Although the weather was bad, (B) but the match (C) went (D) ahead.","o":["Although","but","went","ahead"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"but — Do not use but after although.","h":"لا تجمع although و but."},{"n":67,"s":"He (A) has (B) been working (C) here (D) since five years.","o":["has","been","here","since"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"since — for + period of time.","h":"for خمس سنوات."},{"n":68,"s":"I (A) wish I (B) could speak Japanese (C) more (D) fluent.","o":["wish","could","more","fluent"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"fluent — An adverb is needed: fluently.","h":"الظرف fluently."},{"n":69,"s":"The (A) informations (B) given in the brochure (C) were (D) incorrect.","o":["informations","given","were","incorrect"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"informations — information is uncountable.","h":"information غير معدود."},{"n":70,"s":"Neither (A) of the (B) answers (C) are (D) completely correct.","o":["of","answers","are","completely"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"are — Neither of + singular verb: is.","h":"Neither of مفرد."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M22-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What does the lab assistant think happened to the report?","o":["It was never submitted.","It was uploaded to the wrong system.","Dr. Faisal took it home.","It was put with another subject's reports."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"She will check the physics folder.","h":"وُضع مع تقارير مادة أخرى."}],"t":"A misplaced lab report","sc":["Situation: A student speaks to a lab assistant. (76 words)","Man: Excuse me, I submitted my chemistry lab report on Monday, but the system says it's missing.","Woman: Did you put it in the box outside the lab or upload it online?","Man: I put a printed copy in the box. Dr. Faisal said we could do either.","Woman: Then it may have been placed in the wrong pile. I'll check the physics folder, since they share the same box. If I can't find it, please upload a copy tonight."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M22-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What is the woman suggesting?","o":["The presentation was too long.","The charts were confusing.","The budget figures were wrong.","The audience was not interested in the topic."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"She mentions people checking the clock.","h":"العرض كان طويلًا."}],"t":"A hint that a presentation was too long","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking after a meeting. (64 words)","Man: So, what did you think of my presentation on the new budget?","Woman: The content was excellent, and the charts were very clear. Though I did notice a few people checking the clock towards the end.","Man: Oh. Was it really that long?","Woman: Well, we were scheduled for twenty minutes, and it lasted about forty-five. Maybe next time you could keep the details for the handout."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M22-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"What does Khalid decide to do?","o":["drive his friend to the airport himself","lend him the car for the whole day","refuse to help","pay for a taxi"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'I'll drive you there myself after work'.","h":"يوصله بنفسه."}],"t":"An awkward request to borrow a car","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking. (71 words)","Man: Khalid, I hate to ask, but would there be any chance of borrowing your car on Thursday? Mine's at the garage, and I have to collect my parents from the airport.","Man: Thursday? I need it to get to work, I'm afraid. But I finish at four. What time is their flight?","Man: It lands at six thirty.","Man: Then I'll drive you there myself after work. That's easier than sorting out the insurance."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M22-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"Why has the payment been delayed?","o":["Her scholarship has been cancelled.","Her new bank account has not been verified.","The office forgot to send it.","She did not attend her classes."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated by the officer.","h":"الحساب الجديد غير موثق."}],"t":"A delayed scholarship payment","sc":["Situation: A student speaks to an officer in the scholarships office. (74 words)","Woman: Good morning. My scholarship payment usually arrives on the first of the month, but nothing has come this month.","Man: Let me check. Ah, I see the problem. Your bank account details were updated last week, and the new account hasn't been verified yet.","Woman: I changed banks. Do I need to do anything?","Man: Yes, please bring an official letter from the new bank. Once it's verified, the payment will be transferred within three working days."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M22-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"What is the man's response to the meeting request?","o":["He is politely avoiding a meeting.","He is happy to meet in the morning.","He wants the woman to cancel the plan.","He is asking for more interns."],"k":0,"ty":"function","e":"He offers email comments instead.","h":"يتهرب بلطف."}],"t":"An indirect refusal of a meeting","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking in a corridor. (67 words)","Woman: Could we meet tomorrow afternoon to go through the new marketing plan?","Man: Tomorrow afternoon is rather full, to be honest. I've got the client call, and then I promised to review the interns' reports.","Woman: What about the morning, then?","Man: Hmm, the mornings this week are quite difficult too. Perhaps you could send me the plan by email, and I'll add my comments when I get a chance."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M22-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"Why did the man receive a fine?","o":["He forgot to pay.","He parked for too long.","He paid for the wrong parking zone.","The app was not working."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"The app chose zone fourteen instead of sixteen.","h":"منطقة خاطئة."}],"t":"A confusing parking fine","sc":["Situation: A man is talking to a friend. (78 words)","Man: I've received a parking fine, but I'm sure I paid for parking through the app.","Man: Did you check the zone number? Sometimes the app picks the nearest zone automatically, and it might not be the one you were in.","Man: Let me look. Oh, it says zone fourteen, but the sign said zone sixteen.","Man: That explains it. You can usually appeal online if you include a screenshot of your payment. Since you did pay, they may cancel the fine."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M22-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"Why has the workshop been moved?","o":["More people registered.","The main hall is being repaired.","The speaker requested it.","The library is closed."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"'the main hall is being repaired'.","h":"صيانة القاعة."}],"t":"A last-minute change of venue","sc":["Situation: An organizer speaks to a speaker on the phone. (61 words)","Woman: Dr. Nawaf, I'm calling about tomorrow's workshop. I'm afraid the main hall is being repaired, so we've had to move to the conference room in Building C.","Man: Building C? Is that the one next to the library?","Woman: Yes, on the second floor. It's smaller, so we've limited the number of participants to forty.","Man: That's fine. I'll bring fewer printed handouts, then."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M22-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"What does the tutor advise the student to do first?","o":["remove the student from the group","do the work herself","hold a group meeting and agree tasks in writing","complain to the head of department Longer conversations"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'arranging a short meeting … agreeing clear tasks'.","h":"اجتماع وتحديد المهام."}],"t":"A complaint about a group member","sc":["Situation: A student speaks to her tutor. (69 words)","Woman: Dr. Hind, I don't want to cause trouble, but one member of our group hasn't contributed anything to the project.","Woman: Have you spoken to her about it directly?","Woman: We've sent messages, but she rarely replies.","Woman: I'd suggest arranging a short meeting with the whole group and agreeing clear tasks and deadlines in writing. If she still doesn't take part, come back to me, and I'll take it from there."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M22-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"What do the students think may have affected their results?","o":["the temperature near the window","the type of seeds","the amount of water","the size of the pots"],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"The plants near the window may have been too hot.","h":"الحرارة."},{"n":80,"s":"What do they decide to do?","o":["repeat the experiment","change their results","explain the problem in their report","ask for an extension"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"They will explain it in the discussion section.","h":"شرح المشكلة في التقرير."}],"t":"Discussing the results of a lab experiment","sc":["Situation: Two students are discussing an experiment. (192 words)","Man: Have you looked at the results from our plant growth experiment? The plants with extra fertilizer didn't grow any taller than the control group.","Man: I noticed. That's the opposite of what we predicted.","Man: Do you think we made a mistake?","Man: Possibly. We placed the fertilized plants near the window, didn't we? They might have been too hot in the afternoon sun.","Man: That's true. So temperature could have affected the results, not just the fertilizer.","Man: Exactly. That's what's called an uncontrolled variable. Ideally, all the plants should have been kept in the same conditions.","Man: Should we repeat the experiment?","Man: We don't have time before the deadline. But we can explain the problem honestly in our discussion section and suggest how the experiment could be improved.","Man: Dr. Faisal always says that recognizing the limits of an experiment is part of good science.","Man: Right. A clear explanation of what went wrong might actually earn us marks.","Man: Then let's also include the temperature readings from the window area, if we still have them. They would support our explanation.","Man: I recorded them in my notebook. I'll type them up tonight and add them to our shared file."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M22-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"Why do they decide to arrive on the ninth?","o":["The flights are cheaper that day.","Their presentation is on the tenth.","The hotel is only available then.","The opening session is early on the tenth."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'the opening session starts at nine on the tenth'.","h":"الجلسة الافتتاحية مبكرة."},{"n":82,"s":"How will they choose a hotel?","o":["by choosing the nearest one","by comparing the total cost, including taxis","by asking the organizers","by reading online reviews"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'Let's compare the total cost before deciding.'","h":"مقارنة التكلفة الإجمالية."}],"t":"Planning a conference trip","sc":["Situation: Two researchers are planning a trip. (185 words)","Woman: The conference in Muscat is from the tenth to the twelfth. Shall we book flights for the ninth?","Woman: Yes, the opening session starts at nine on the tenth, so it's safer to arrive the day before.","Woman: What about the return? Our presentation is on the last afternoon.","Woman: Then let's fly back on the morning of the thirteenth. I'd rather not rush to the airport straight after presenting.","Woman: Agreed. The conference recommends two hotels. One is next to the venue but quite expensive. The other is cheaper but twenty minutes away by taxi.","Woman: Our travel budget is limited, but taxis every day will add up. Let's compare the total cost before deciding.","Woman: Good idea. And we need to register by Friday to get the early-bird discount.","Woman: I'll register us both today. Can you check whether we need visas?","Woman: As Saudi citizens we don't, but I'll confirm on the embassy website.","Woman: Thanks. And let's rehearse our presentation together next week. The organizers have given each speaker only fifteen minutes, including questions, so we'll need to cut some slides.","Woman: Good point. I'll book a meeting room for Tuesday."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M22-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"What is the main advantage of interviews, according to the man?","o":["They reach more students.","They are quicker to carry out.","They provide richer, more detailed information.","They are easier to analyse."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"People explain in their own words.","h":"معلومات أعمق."},{"n":84,"s":"What does the man recommend?","o":["using only interviews","using only a survey","changing the research topic","combining a survey with a few interviews Talks and lectures"],"k":3,"ty":"opinion","e":"'Why not combine them?'","h":"الجمع بين الطريقتين."}],"t":"Choosing between two research methods","sc":["Situation: A student discusses her research with a classmate. (185 words)","Woman: I'm researching why students choose particular university majors, and I can't decide between interviews and an online survey.","Man: What are you most interested in finding out?","Woman: Mainly the reasons behind their choices: family influence, job prospects, personal interest and so on.","Man: Then interviews might give you richer information. People can explain their reasons in their own words, and you can ask follow-up questions.","Woman: True, but interviews take a long time. I could only manage about fifteen, and my supervisor says that's not enough to generalize.","Man: A survey would reach hundreds of students, but the answers would be more limited. Why not combine them? Start with a survey, then interview a small number of students to explore the most interesting results in depth.","Woman: That's a mixed-methods approach. It would take more planning, though.","Man: Yes, but it would make your conclusions much stronger. Just make sure the survey questions are tested on a few people first.","Woman: You mean a pilot study? That's a good idea. Unclear questions would ruin the data.","Man: Exactly. And ask your supervisor to look at the interview questions as well before you start."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M22-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"Why is forecasting useful for airports?","o":["It reduces the price of tickets.","It replaces security checks.","It increases the number of flights.","It allows staff to be scheduled before queues form."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"Planners can act before queues build up.","h":"التخطيط المسبق."},{"n":86,"s":"Why does the lecturer mention the airport in Jeddah?","o":["to show that it has no security checks","to give an example of managing very large numbers of pilgrims","to compare its shops with other airports","to explain how planes are repaired"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"A separate terminal helps during Hajj.","h":"مثال على إدارة أعداد الحجاج."},{"n":87,"s":"According to research, when do waits feel shorter?","o":["when passengers are kept occupied","when queues are very long","when passengers stand still","when there are no screens"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'people feel waits are shorter when they are occupied'.","h":"عند الانشغال."}],"t":"Extended lecture: how airports manage passenger flow","sc":["Situation: A lecturer speaks to students of logistics. (284 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today, we're looking at how large airports manage the movement of passengers. At peak times, a major airport may handle tens of thousands of people every hour, and each one must pass through several stages: check-in, security, passport control and boarding. If any stage becomes a bottleneck, delays spread quickly through the whole system. The first tool airports use is forecasting. By analysing flight schedules and historical data, planners can predict how many passengers will arrive at each stage and when. This allows them to schedule the right number of staff and open extra security lanes before queues build up, rather than reacting once they have already formed. The second tool is technology that spreads the load. Online check-in and self-service bag-drop machines reduce pressure on check-in desks, while automated passport gates can process travellers far faster than traditional booths. At King Abdulaziz International Airport in Jeddah, which must cope with huge numbers of pilgrims during the Hajj season, a separate terminal and dedicated procedures help to keep these flows apart from regular traffic. The third tool is design. Wide corridors, clear signs and logical layouts reduce confusion, and studies show that passengers who can see where they are going walk faster and make fewer mistakes. Some airports now use sensors and cameras to measure waiting times in real time and display them on screens, which encourages passengers to choose shorter queues. Finally, airports pay increasing attention to the passenger experience. Interestingly, research suggests that people feel waits are shorter when they are occupied, so shops, seating and information screens are placed strategically along the route. In short, efficient passenger flow depends on accurate prediction, smart technology and thoughtful design working together."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M22-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"Which type of memory often continues to improve in later life?","o":["working memory","episodic memory","semantic memory","short-term memory for numbers"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"General knowledge and vocabulary improve.","h":"الذاكرة الدلالية."},{"n":89,"s":"Why is sleep mentioned in the lecture?","o":["It causes memory loss.","It improves working memory immediately.","It is unrelated to memory.","It helps to strengthen new memories."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'sleep plays an important role in strengthening new memories'.","h":"تقوية الذكريات."},{"n":90,"s":"What is the lecturer's main message about memory and age?","o":["All memory declines quickly with age.","Some memory declines, but much remains stable and lifestyle helps.","Older adults cannot learn new skills.","Forgetting keys is a sign of illness."],"k":1,"ty":"gist","e":"She stresses the complexity and the good news.","h":"الصورة أعقد مما يظن الناس."}],"t":"Lecture: how memory changes with age","sc":["Situation: A psychology lecturer speaks to students. (286 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. Today's topic is how memory changes as we grow older. Many people assume that memory simply declines with age, but the reality is more complex. Different types of memory are affected in different ways. Let's start with working memory, the ability to hold and manipulate information for a short time, such as remembering a phone number while dialling it. Working memory tends to decline gradually from early adulthood. Older adults also tend to process information more slowly, which can make tasks that require quick responses more challenging. Episodic memory, our memory for specific personal experiences, such as what we ate for dinner last Tuesday, also shows a decline, particularly for details like when or where something happened. However, other types of memory remain remarkably stable. Semantic memory, our general knowledge of facts and vocabulary, often continues to improve well into later life. Older adults typically know more words than younger adults and perform better on tests of general knowledge. Procedural memory, our memory for skills such as riding a bicycle or typing, is also well preserved. So why does some memory decline? One reason is that the brain's frontal regions, which support attention and organization, change with age. Another is reduced sleep quality, since sleep plays an important role in strengthening new memories. The good news is that lifestyle makes a real difference. Regular physical exercise increases blood flow to the brain, and several studies have linked it to better memory performance. Staying socially active, continuing to learn new skills and getting enough sleep are also associated with healthier memory. It's also worth noting that occasional forgetfulness, such as misplacing keys, is normal at any age and is not necessarily a sign of illness."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 3 – 4 – 1","3 – 2 – 4 – 1","2 – 4 – 3 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4"],"x":["(1) Finally, the dried leaves are sorted and packed for sale.","(2) Tea production begins with picking young leaves by hand.","(3) The leaves are then spread out to wither for several hours.","(4) Next, they are rolled and dried in hot air."],"k":0,"ty":"best order","e":"begins → then → Next → Finally.","h":"ترتيب المراحل."},{"n":92,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["However","Otherwise","In contrast","For instance"],"x":["Public transport in the city has improved greatly. ______, the new metro has reduced travel times between the airport and the city centre."],"k":3,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second sentence gives an example.","h":"مثال."},{"n":93,"s":"(A) Everyone in the class (B) have (C) submitted (D) their project.","o":["Everyone","have","submitted","their"],"k":1,"ty":"error","e":"Everyone takes a singular verb: has.","h":"Everyone مفرد."},{"n":94,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1","2 – 4 – 3 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4"],"x":["(1) This makes it difficult for them to concentrate the next day.","(2) Many teenagers use their phones late at night.","(3) The light from screens can delay sleep.","(4) As a result, they often get less sleep than they need."],"k":1,"ty":"best order","e":"cause → effect → result → further effect.","h":"سبب ثم نتيجة."},{"n":95,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Consequently","Nevertheless","Similarly","For example"],"x":["The company's profits fell sharply last year. ______, it has decided to close two of its branches."],"k":0,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second sentence is a result.","h":"Consequently للنتيجة."},{"n":96,"s":"The (A) recently published report (B) shows that pollution (C) have fallen (D) significantly.","o":["recently","shows","have","significantly"],"k":2,"ty":"error","e":"pollution is singular: has.","h":"pollution مفرد."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Dates are an important part of Saudi culture. (2) They are traditionally offered to guests with Arabic coffee. (3) Saudi Arabia produces hundreds of varieties of dates. (4) Coffee beans are grown in many tropical countries. (5) Dates are also an important source of energy and fibre."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Where coffee is grown does not develop the point about dates.","h":"زراعة القهوة خارج الفكرة."},{"n":98,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Regular exercise has many benefits for physical and mental health.","Many gyms are open late.","Heart disease is common worldwide.","Some people prefer team sports."],"x":["______ It reduces stress, improves sleep and lowers the risk of heart disease. In addition, it can improve mood and concentration."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details list benefits of exercise.","h":"فوائد الرياضة."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Many shops have websites.","Delivery drivers work long hours.","Shoppers should therefore weigh these advantages and disadvantages before deciding how to buy.","Some products are cheaper in stores."],"x":["Online shopping offers convenience and a wide choice of products. Customers can compare prices quickly and have items delivered to their homes. However, they cannot touch or try products before buying, and returns can be inconvenient. ______"],"k":2,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A balanced conclusion.","h":"خلاصة متوازنة."},{"n":100,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Shopping malls are air-conditioned.","Many people shop online.","Some souqs close at noon.","They offer local products, such as spices, perfumes and textiles, that are hard to find elsewhere. Teacher Section — Mock 22"],"x":["Traditional markets, or souqs, remain popular in many Saudi cities. ______ In addition, they preserve traditional crafts and give visitors a sense of the country's history."],"k":3,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"A first reason before 'In addition'.","h":"سبب أول قبل In addition."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
